# DESCRIPTION

The core is what a native GPU driver implements and what its clients are written on: Loinnir (loinnir(2)) and the Vulkan layer.
It has one model, whatever a GPU can do:

- one bindless heap;
- one root pointer;
- command lists as data;
- image layouts that are the device's own;
- one timeline.

What a GPU can do, the core says, and a backend that lacks something says so in `DeviceFeatures` or `DeviceLimits`.
It names no graphics API.
It is Swift, tier 0.
A backend is a class that conforms to `GPUDevice` and is its own `GPUQueue`; there are two:

- `VulkanDevice` (graphics/core/vulkan) runs the core over Vulkan: Venus's under QEMU, or a host's own.
- `MaliDevice` (graphics/core/mali) runs it on a Mali GPU's command streams, through the `csf` capset of the GPU class (mali(3)), with MaliCore's recipes and the compiler's Valhall backend (valhallbackend(2)).

A program that wants Loinnir's whole library takes `Loinnir`, which picks the backend by what the GPU class offers: a GPU that offers `csf` gets `MaliDevice`; any other, `VulkanDevice` over Venus.

Every addition to the core has a default that means what the core did without it: a new field of a descriptor, a new argument of a call, a new requirement of `GPUDevice`.
A requirement a backend doesn't implement refuses with `.unsupported`.
What a client wrote before an addition compiles and runs as it did.

## What is refused, and how

An error is of one of two kinds, and they stay apart.
`.invalid` is a shape that is wrong on every device: a region outside its image, formats a copy can't go between, a draw outside a pass, a depth test with no depth attachment.
`.unsupported` is a shape that is right, which this device can't do: a feature its `DeviceFeatures` lack, a limit it is past, or a format it doesn't offer for that use.
Shape is judged first, so a list or a descriptor that one device runs and another refuses is the other's refusal of a feature, never of its shape.
The words of the error say what.

The rules are written once, in the core, and every backend calls them: `GPUCommandList.validate` for lists, and `check(limits:)` of `ImageDescriptor`, `SamplerDescriptor`, `GraphicsPipelineDescriptor` and `ImageView` (`check(for:limits:)`) where a backend makes the thing.
`validate(features:)` judges a list by the features alone, and `validate(limits:)` also by the limits and the formats' capabilities (a blit's formats, a pass's sample counts).

## Memory

`allocate` gives memory by what it is for: `.upload` is written by the CPU and read by the GPU, `.device` is the GPU's alone, `.readback` is written by the GPU and read by the CPU.
A `GPUMemory` has a 64-bit GPU `address`, which shaders follow, and for `.upload` and `.readback` a CPU `pointer` to the same bytes.
An image with the `scanout` usage (a render target, never sampled) is one a display shows as it is: `exportHandle(_ image:)` gives a new handle to its VMO, for the display to pin and read (the rights to transfer, read and measure it), where its rows are `width * 4` bytes; the Mali backend maps it as the GPU and the display share it, and does no cache maintenance over it.
On the Mali backend `.readback` memory, and an image that is drawn into and copied out of and never copied into, are mapped cached by the CPU, so reading them is as fast as memory; the backend cleans and invalidates their caches when a submission whose last step is the CPU's is done, and in `wait(_:)`, so what the CPU reads after either is what the GPU wrote.
`free` and every `destroy` are safe at any time: the device keeps the resource until the work submitted before the call has finished.

`importHandle` is the other half of `exportHandle`: it makes memory, or an image, over the pages of a handle (a VMO) another process gave, with the device mapping its own reference.
It is `DeviceFeatures.importHandle`; where the device can't, it is `.unsupported`.
A window's frames, drawn by one process and composed by another, move this way: one VMO by ownership, never a copy of its pixels.

`makePresentImage(format:width:height:usage:direct:)` makes the image a window shows (`PresentImage`): the one a program draws into, with `usage` and the copy source a present needs, and the VMO the window is given (`exportHandle(_:)` of the `PresentImage`, a new handle each call).
The format is a four-byte colour format; Radharc reads blue first, so `bgra8` and `bgra8srgb` are the ones that show right.
There are two ways to the VMO.
Where the device exports the image's own pages and the caller says the window's reader can use them (`direct`, which the callers don't yet, because an image's export is a VMO to pin and read and not to map), the image is `scanout` and `isDirect`, and presenting it adds nothing.
Otherwise `recordPresent(into:)` adds the GPU's copy of the image into host-visible memory (`staging`, `width * height * 4` bytes, rows of `width` pixels) that the device exports, and the window is given that: the way under Venus, where an image's pages are the host's, in its tiling.
The call throws `.unsupported` if the device exports neither, as the Mali backend today, which has no `exportHandle` of memory.
`destroy(_ frame:)` gives the image and its staging memory back.

## Formats

A `PixelFormat` names a texel's layout.
The core has:

- colour: `r8`, `rg8` and `rgba8` in unorm, snorm, uint and sint (the unorm ones are `r8`, `rg8` and `rgba8`, the others `r8snorm`, `rg8uint` and so on), `rgba8srgb`, `bgra8` and `bgra8srgb`; `r16`, `rg16` and `rgba16` in float, uint and sint; `r32`, `rg32` and `rgba32` in float, uint and sint; `rgb10a2unorm`, `rg11b10float` and `rgb9e5`;
- depth and stencil: `depth16`, `depth32f`, `depth24stencil8`, `depth32fstencil8` and `stencil8`;
- compressed: `astc` and `astcSRGB` of each of the fourteen `ASTCBlock` sizes, ETC2 and EAC (`etc2rgb8`, `etc2rgb8a1`, `etc2rgba8` and their sRGB forms, `eacR11` and `eacRG11`, each unorm or snorm), and the BC formats (`bc1rgb` to `bc7srgb`).

A format describes itself.
`texelBlock` is the block's width and height in texels and its size in bytes (one texel, for an uncompressed format), as the Vulkan specification gives them; a combined depth and stencil texel is the whole texel's size (`depth32fstencil8` is five bytes), and `bytesPerTexel(aspect:)` is one aspect's, as a copy that names an aspect counts.
`aspects` says what a texel holds (`color`, `depth`, `stencil`), `isCompressed` and `family` which compressed family it is in, `numericType` how a channel is read, and `byteSize(width:height:depth:aspect:)` how many bytes a tightly packed extent takes, whole blocks to a row.
`bytesPerPixel` is a texel's bytes for an uncompressed colour format, and 0 for a compressed one.

`DeviceLimits.formats(_:)` is what a device does with a format: `FormatCapabilities` says whether it can be sampled, filtered, a render target, blended into, a storage image, a storage image with atomics, a depth target, or the source or destination of a blit, and the sample counts it has for each use (always including one).
A format the device doesn't list has none of these, and no image of it is made.
A backend fills the table at start (`DeviceLimits.setFormat`).
Which of the compressed families (ASTC, ETC2, BC) a device has is `DeviceFeatures`, apart from each format's capabilities.

## Images and views

An image has a format, a size (`width`, `height` and, for a 3D image, `depth`), `mipLevels`, `layers`, `samples` and a `dimension` (`d2`, `d3`, `cube` or `d1`; a cube is a 2D array whose layers are six to a cube, and the image is square; a 1D image is one row of texels, with layers where it is an array), and a usage: `sampled`, `renderTarget` (a colour attachment), `depthStencilTarget`, `storage`, `copySource`, `copyDestination` and `scanout`.
An image of one mip level, one layer and one sample, as a 2D image, is the image the core had; the other fields default to it, and the device refuses (`.unsupported`) what it lacks: mips, layers, cubes, volumes, multisampling, a depth or compressed format.
A multisampled image is 2D, has one mip level and an uncompressed format.
A 1D image is `DeviceFeatures.images1D`; its height and depth are 1, it has one sample and a colour format that isn't compressed, and it is sampled, stored to and copied but never drawn into, so a device that has them takes `sampled`, `storage`, `copySource` and `copyDestination` of it and refuses (`.invalid`) a render target, a depth target or a scanout.
A device picks the layout and tiling and tracks the layout itself: nothing at the interface names one.
Sampled images rest ready to sample.

An `ImageView` is an image, a format compatible with it, a range of its mip levels, a range of its layers, a dimension (`d2`, `d2Array`, `cube`, `cubeArray`, `d3`, `d1`, `d1Array`), the aspects it reads and a component mapping; `ImageView(image)` is the whole of it.
A view of a depth and stencil image that is sampled reads one aspect.
A view of a 1D image is `d1` (one layer) or `d1Array`, and one of another image's is never 1D.
The component mapping (`ComponentMapping`: `r`, `g`, `b` and `a`, each `identity`, `zero`, `one` or a letter) is how the view's four components are read by a shader: the texel's own (the identity, the default), a constant, or another of the texel's.
A mapped view is `DeviceFeatures.imageSwizzle`; a view that maps nothing (each component the identity or its own letter) is the whole image, as the image-only calls mean it, and is taken everywhere.
A mapping is for sampling: a storage view and an attachment that map components are `.invalid`, and no attachment is a 1D view.
The heap's `writeTexture` and `writeStorageImage` and a pass's attachments take a view; the calls that take an image mean the whole image, and a device that has no views (`DeviceFeatures.imageViews`) takes only those.

There is one bindless heap for each device, of five arrays: textures, samplers and storage images, and the uniform texel buffers and the storage texel buffers (below).
The caller owns the indices: it writes a slot with `writeTexture`, `writeSampler`, `writeStorageImage`, `writeTexelBuffer` or `writeStorageTexelBuffer`, and a shader names the slot.
A slot may be written again at any time; work already submitted keeps the descriptor it was submitted with.
`limits` says how many slots each array has.

## Texel buffers

A `BufferView` is a range of memory read as an array of texels of one format: the memory, an offset in it, a range in bytes (a whole number of texels; to the end of the memory where not said) and a format that is neither depth nor compressed.
A shader fetches from a uniform texel buffer (`samplerBuffer`, by `OpImageFetch`: no sampler, no filtering, no mip levels) and reads, writes and applies atomics to a storage texel buffer (`imageBuffer`).
`DeviceFeatures.uniformTexelBuffers` and `storageTexelBuffers` say whether the device has each kind, `FormatCapabilities.texelBuffer`, `storageTexelBuffer` and `storageTexelBufferAtomic` which formats, `DeviceLimits.heapTexelBuffers` and `heapStorageTexelBuffers` how many slots each array has, and `maxTexelBufferElements` the most texels a view covers.
`BufferView.check(for:limits:)` is the rule: a range that isn't whole texels, that begins before or ends past its memory, or a depth or compressed format, is `.invalid`; the features, the format's capability and the most texels are `.unsupported`.
The texel buffers are two arrays of their own in the heap, not slots among the textures and the storage images: a Vulkan device holds a buffer view and an image view in descriptors of different types, which no one array can hold, and a Mali heap's resource table has an entry for each in its own right, so a separate index space costs a device that could hold both nothing and spares the other a translation.
The arrays are bindings 3 (uniform) and 4 (storage) of the heap; an `OpImageTexelPointer` on a storage texel buffer is the atomics' address.
A device that has none keeps the default of `writeTexelBuffer` and `writeStorageTexelBuffer`, which refuse (`.unsupported`).

A sampler has a minification and a magnification filter (`filter` sets both), a mip mode, an address mode on each of the three axes (`clampToEdge`, `repeat`, `mirroredRepeat`, `mirrorClampToEdge`, `clampToBorder`; `addressMode` sets all three) and a border colour (one of six, or a `CustomBorderColor` of the sampler's own: four floats, or four integers, `DeviceFeatures.customBorderColor`, which is `.invalid` where no axis clamps to the border), a bias, a minimum and a maximum level of detail, an anisotropy, an optional comparison operation, and unnormalized coordinates.
The default sampler is the one the core had: linear, clamped, no mip levels.
A sampler that asks for what the device lacks is `.unsupported`, and one whose fields contradict each other (unnormalized coordinates with mip levels, say) is `.invalid`.

## Pipelines

`makeGraphicsPipeline` takes SPIR-V for a vertex and a fragment stage, the colour formats and the fixed state; `makeComputePipeline` takes one SPIR-V module.
The device's own compiler makes the pipeline.
Every pipeline has the one layout: the heap, and a 64-bit root pointer, which is the first eight bytes of the push constants, as a `PushConstant` block of one buffer reference.
A draw or a dispatch passes the root pointer, the GPU address of whatever the shaders read; they reach textures by the indices that data holds.
There is no vertex input state and no descriptor set to state.

A graphics pipeline's fixed state is, apart from the formats:

- `blend`, `opaque` or source alpha over, which names the blend of every target; or `attachmentBlends`, a `ColorBlendAttachment` for each (every `BlendingFactor`, a `BlendOp` for colour and for alpha, and a `ColorMask`), with `logicOp` and `blendConstants`;
- `depthStencilFormat` and `depthStencil`: the depth test, write and `CompareOp`, the depth bounds test and its bounds, and the stencil test with a `StencilFaceState` for each face (fail, pass and depth-fail `StencilOp`s, compare, masks and reference);
- `rasterization`: cull (none, front, back, both), front face, polygon mode (fill, line, point), depth bias, depth clamp, line width and rasterizer discard;
- `multisample`: a sample count, a sample mask, alpha to coverage, alpha to one and sample shading;
- `topology`: the lists and strips, `triangleFan`, `lineStrip` and `patchList`, with `primitiveRestart` and `patchControlPoints`;
- `geometry`, `tessellationControl` and `tessellationEvaluation`, the stages beyond the two, where the device has them;
- `specialization`: a value for each of the modules' specialization constants, by id;
- `viewMask`, for a multiview pass; and
- `dynamic`: which of the blend constants, the stencil reference and masks, the depth bias, the depth bounds and the line width the pass's commands set in place of the descriptor's.

Each is today's behaviour by default: no depth or stencil, no culling, filled polygons, one sample, the blend `blend` names.
A pipeline's `targets` are the attachments it draws into; a backend that says so (`GPUPipeline.targets`) has its draws held to the pass they are in.

## Command lists

A `GPUCommandList` is data: commands recorded with no device, and replayed by the backend at `submit`.
It has compute dispatches (direct and indirect), passes with dynamic rendering, draws (direct, indexed, indirect, and indirect with a count), copies, clears, blits and resolves, barriers, and queries.

A pass is `beginRendering` with the attachments, each saying what happens to its contents at the pass's start (`load`, `clear` or `dontCare`) and end (`store` or `discard`), as a tiler needs before the pass begins; `endRendering`; and the draws between.
`RenderingInfo` has the colour attachments, each a view, with an optional single-sample view to resolve into; an optional depth and stencil attachment with a load and a store for each aspect and its own clear value; a layer count; a view mask for multiview; and a render area.
The viewport and scissor start as the whole of the first attachment.

The copies are: memory to memory; memory to an image and an image to memory, whole (mip level 0 of layer 0, rows tightly packed) or by regions, each with a mip level, layers, an offset, an extent, a buffer offset and row length; image to image (`copyImage`, between formats of one class); `blitImage`, which scales regions with a nearest or linear filter and flips where the corners are swapped; `resolveImage`, from a multisampled image into a single-sampled one of its format; `fill` and `update` of memory.
A region lies inside its subresource, and where the format is compressed it starts on a block and ends on one or on the image's edge.
`clear` an image (a colour, whose type is the format's: float, unsigned or signed; or depth and stencil) and `clearAttachments` inside a pass.
Inside a pass, `setBlendConstants`, `setStencilReference`, `setStencilCompareMask`, `setStencilWriteMask`, `setDepthBias`, `setDepthBounds` and `setLineWidth` set the state a pipeline's `dynamic` leaves to them.
`drawIndirectCount` and `drawIndexedIndirectCount` take the number of draws from memory, up to a maximum.

A barrier names stages only (`compute`, `vertex`, `fragment`, `transfer`): the earlier stages' writes are visible to the later stages' reads and writes.
A barrier that names an image and a range of its subresources is accepted, for the backends that can use the range; a device that can't treats it as the barrier without it.

`validate` is the one place the list's rules are written, and every backend holds a list to it before it runs.
They include the old ones (a draw outside a pass, a dispatch inside one, a copy past its memory, a copy into an image that isn't a copy destination, a pass that never ends) and these:

- an attachment is a render target (or a depth and stencil target, of a depth or stencil format); all the attachments are of one size and one sample count; a view of an attachment is one mip level; a resolve target matches its attachment's format and size and has one sample; a multiview pass has one layer count; the render area is inside the attachments;
- a draw's pipeline, where it says what it draws into, matches the pass;
- a copy, blit, resolve or clear names a usage the image has, regions inside the image, formats that are compatible, a sample count that fits, and an aspect the format has; a clear colour has the type of the format;
- a state command is inside a pass, and its values are in range; an indirect draw's arguments and count are inside their memory and aligned;
- a query is of the kind its set is, and its index is in the set.

A list that breaks them is `.invalid`; a list that needs what the device can't do is `.unsupported`.

## Features and limits

`DeviceLimits.features` has a flag for each optional capability, all false until a backend says otherwise:

- images: `depth`, `stencil`, `mipmaps`, `arrayImages`, `cubeImages`, `volumeImages`, `images1D`, `multisample`, `compressedASTC`, `compressedETC2`, `compressedBC`, `imageViews`, `imageSwizzle`;
- texel buffers: `uniformTexelBuffers`, `storageTexelBuffers`;
- samplers: `samplerAnisotropy`, `samplerCompare`, `samplerBorder`, `unnormalizedCoordinates`, `samplerMinMagFilters`, `samplerAxisAddressModes`, `samplerMirrorClampToEdge`, `customBorderColor`;
- pipeline state: `cullModes`, `rasterizerDiscard`, `generalBlend`, `independentBlend`, `dualSourceBlend`, `logicOps`, `fillModeNonSolid`, `wideLines`, `depthClamp`, `depthBias`, `depthBounds`, `geometryShader`, `tessellation`, `multiview`, `extendedTopologies`, `primitiveRestart`, `sampleShading`, `alphaToCoverage`, `alphaToOne`, `specializationConstants`, `dynamicState`, `multipleColorAttachments`;
- commands: `copyImage`, `blitImage`, `resolveImage`, `resolveAttachments`, `depthStencilResolve`, `imageRegionCopies`, `fillBuffer`, `updateBuffer`, `clearImage`, `clearAttachments`, `drawIndirectCount`, `pipelineStatisticsQueries`, `importHandle`;
- what shaders may do (SPIR-V's capabilities): `shaderFloat16`, `shaderInt8`, `shaderInt16`, `shaderInt64`, `shaderFloat64`, `subgroupOperations`, `shaderAtomic32`, `shaderAtomic64`, `shaderImageAtomics`, `shaderDerivatives`, the storage image formats, clip and cull distances, gather, minimum level of detail, draw parameters, demote, layer and viewport index, stencil export, sample-rate shading and float controls;
- the ones the core had: `computeQueue`, `indirectDispatch`, `indirectDraw`, `indexedDraw`, `sampledImages`, `storageImages`, `timestampQueries`, `occlusionQueries`, `loadAttachments`, `multipleDrawsPerPass`.

The limits are the heap's slots, `maxImageDimension` (and for 3D, `maxImageDimension3D`), `maxImageArrayLayers`, `maxSamples`, `maxAnisotropy`, `maxLineWidth`, `maxColorAttachments`, `maxMultiviewViews`, `maxPatchControlPoints`, `subgroupSize` and the timestamp period.

## The timeline

A device has one timeline: a 64-bit counter that only goes up, which the GPU class's counters are (counter(2)).
`submit` waits until the timeline reaches `wait` (0 for no wait) and sets it to `signal` when the list is done; `signal` must be greater than any value the device has signalled, and `wait` must be a value some earlier submission signalled (`TimelineRule` keeps these).
Lists on one queue run in the order submitted.
`completed` is the highest value reached and `wait(_:)` waits for one.
A `.readback` allocation's bytes are the CPU's to read once the timeline has reached the value the submission that wrote them signalled.

## Queries

`makeQuerySet` makes a set of timestamps (in ticks of `limits.timestampPeriodPicoseconds`), of occlusion counts, or of pipeline statistics (the counters `PipelineStatistics` names); `writeTimestamp` and `beginQuery` and `endQuery` fill it and `results` reads it after the work has completed.
A statistics set's results hold one value for each counter of each query, in the order of the counters' bits.

## The Vulkan backend

`VulkanDevice` is Loinnir's Vulkan code behind the core: one device with dynamic rendering, buffer device addresses, descriptor indexing and timeline semaphores, and the layout transitions a list needs, made as it replays the list into a command buffer.
Memory is a buffer with a device address; the heap is one descriptor set (sampled images at binding 0, samplers at binding 1, 16 of them, and with `storageImages: true`, where the device offers the features, storage images at binding 2, and with `texelBuffers: true`, where it offers its update-after-bind and non-uniform indexing features, 1024 uniform texel buffers at binding 3 and 1024 storage texel buffers at binding 4).
It implements the whole of the core, as far as the device under it does: each `DeviceFeatures` flag and each `DeviceLimits` value is read from what the physical device reports, at start, and nothing is assumed.
The features the core names are asked of the device (the 1.0 set, and the 1.1, 1.2 and 1.3 sets) and the ones it has are enabled; the properties give the sizes and the sample counts.
A flag for what Vulkan 1.3 requires of every device (the backend's device needs 1.3's dynamic rendering) is set when the device reports 1.3 or later, and one for an optional feature when the device has it: independent and dual-source blending, logic operations, the non-fill polygon modes, wide lines, depth clamp, depth bounds, geometry and tessellation shaders, sample shading, anisotropy, the compressed families (ASTC, ETC2, BC), multiview, counted draws, pipeline statistics, the 8-, 16- and 64-bit shader types, 64-bit atomics, and the shader capabilities the rest of the flags name.
`depth` and `stencil` are set when some depth or stencil format is a depth target, `multisample` when the device has colour attachments of more than one sample, `arrayImages` and `volumeImages` from the limits of layers and of a 3D side, and the transfer commands (image copies, blits, resolves, regions, fills, updates, clears) when the queue the backend uses does graphics.
`alphaToOne`, `depthStencilResolve` and `importHandle` are false: the backend doesn't enable alpha to one or resolve a depth attachment, and Venus implements only the exporting side of memory sharing (`exportHandle` works), so an import is `.unsupported`.
`formats(_:)` is filled from the device's own format properties and sample counts, one entry for each format the device can sample, draw into, store to, blit or use as a depth target.
A list is replayed into a command buffer with each image's layout tracked from the commands that use it.
A pass is a dynamic rendering pass, with a depth and stencil attachment, a resolve for each colour attachment, layers and a view mask.
The pipeline state of the core (depth and stencil, rasterization, multisampling, blending for each attachment and logic operations, the dynamic states, the topologies, the optional stages and specialization constants) is the pipeline's, one to one; a specialization constant is 32 bits, or 64 where its value has bits past the 32nd.
A heap write of a view keeps a view of the image (a format, a range of mip levels and layers, a dimension: 1D, 1D array, 2D, array, cube, cube array or 3D, and the component mapping, one to one), made once and cached, and a sampler is the heap's own, one to one.
A 1D image is a 1D Vulkan image, and a heap write of a `BufferView` makes a `VkBufferView` of the allocation's buffer at the offset, range and format, kept until the allocation is freed (the offset must be a multiple of the device's `minTexelBufferOffsetAlignment`, which a view made at another is refused for, `.unsupported`); every allocation can be a texel buffer.
A sampler's own border colour is `VK_EXT_custom_border_color`'s, with no format named, taken where the host's Vulkan has the extension and both of its features; `customBorderColor` is set from them.
Under Venus the host's extensions are Venus's driver's own and none of the renderer's (VK_EXT_custom_border_color is not among them), so the flag is false there, and the texel-buffer features the heap needs (update after bind and non-uniform indexing of the two kinds) and the 1D and mapped views are whatever the host reports.
The queries are occlusion, timestamp and pipeline statistics, and `validate` judges a list against the device's limits before it is replayed.
Everything the device lacks it refuses at the call that makes the image, the sampler or the pipeline, or at `submit`, with `.unsupported`.
loinnir-test(8) prints which flags the device behind Venus reports, and draws, copies and judges each of these against a CPU reference.
On the dev machine it opens the host's Vulkan, or ours over Venus and vtest when `TODHCHAI_VULKAN_LIBRARY` names `libtdvulkan.so`.

## The Mali backend

`MaliDevice` runs on one VM of a `csf` context: each allocation is a buffer bound at a page-aligned address, and a list becomes steps.
A compute dispatch and a render pass of its draws are MaliCore's recipes (ComputeDispatch and RenderPass) laid out in memory of the VM, with the pipelines compiled from SPIR-V when they are made (compute shaders may not name descriptor buffers: the core's layout is the heap and a root pointer).
A pass with no draw, and every copy, is done by the CPU, in order with the passes.
It runs one submission at a time: a submission starts when the one before it is done.
`MaliTransport` is what it needs of the GPU's streams interface; `CSFTransport` is that interface over the `csf` capset's client, and a test can give another.
What it can do now: memory by intent; linear `rgba8`, `bgra8`, `bgra8srgb` and `r8` images, which a render pass writes and the texture unit reads in the same layout; the heap's textures, samplers and storage images; compute pipelines and dispatches, direct and indirect; graphics pipelines for `rgba8`, `bgra8` or `bgra8srgb` targets, and passes of up to sixteen draws, direct, indexed, from a first vertex or instance, and indirect (a draw count of the command, its arguments read from memory by the stream when it runs), whose attachment is cleared, `dontCare` or kept at its start; timestamp queries.
An indexed draw gives the tiler the index data's address and size, its type (`uint16` or `uint32`), the first index and the base vertex (signed, added to each index); the vertex shader's `VertexIndex` is the index plus the base vertex, and a draw from a first vertex has the first vertex in it.
An indirect draw's four words (vertex count, instance count, first vertex, first instance), or an indexed one's five, are loaded into the run's count registers by the stream, which waits for the load before it issues the run; each of `drawCount` draws is its own run, `stride` bytes on, and they count toward the sixteen of a pass.
A draw whose vertex or index count or instance count is zero is not issued: the stream branches over the run when the register is zero, since what the hardware does with a zero count in a run is not stated.
An indirect dispatch loads its three words into the run's grid registers (`JOB_SIZE_X/Y/Z`) and into the shader's number-of-workgroups words, and issues RUN_COMPUTE on the X axis with the largest increment a task holds, as the hardware's indirect form is not used; workgroup memory is sized for the largest grid a core can hold.
A dispatch with a zero in any of the three words is not issued either (three branches over the run); the shader never runs and the stream goes on to the next step.
A pass that keeps its attachment (`load`) has a first pre-frame shader, run once for each tile before the pass's draws: it fetches the target's texel at the pixel and writes it as the colour, in a heap of its own made for the pass (the nearest sampler and the target as a texture), and the pass's opaque blend stores it in the tile as it is; a second pass over an image keeps what the first drew.
A pass that keeps its attachment and draws nothing is no work.
A timestamp is the system timer's counter stored by a stream of the compute queue once every step before it has finished, so the stages a `writeTimestamp` names make no difference.
The GPU's counter runs at the frequency of the CPU's architected counter, so the tick is the inverse of what `CNTFRQ_EL0` reads (`MaliTransport.counterFrequency`; 1000 ps on the Orange Pi 6 Plus, which two stores 40 ms apart against the system clock also measure); only where that register reads 0 does the first read of `limits` measure it, with two small streams, and a GPU whose counter can't be read then has no timestamp queries.
An occlusion query is the draws' 64-bit counter: a draw's counter mode (draw flags 0 `[15:14]` 3) and the query's address in the run's `OQ` register make the hardware add the samples that passed into it, in its caches.
The memory of an occlusion query set is therefore mapped cacheable for the GPU (`MaliTransport.bind`'s `gpuCacheable`; in write-combining memory the count stays 0).
The pass's fragment queue stores a zero over each report that the pass begins a query on, before its fragment work and followed by a clean flush; its tail's clean flush, which waits for the fragment work, puts the counts in memory before the submission signals, so the CPU reads them with no maintenance.
A query begun in a pass counts the samples of the draws between `beginQuery` and `endQuery` that pass the tests, and a pass with no draw zeroes its queries on the CPU.
A storage image is a texture descriptor of one level with the identity swizzle over a generic linear plane, in the storage array that is table 2 of the resource table (1024 slots, apart from the textures' and samplers'); the shaders' handle for element 0 of binding 2 of set 0 is table 2, index 0, and a dispatch or a pass whose shaders name a storage image is given a copy of the array as it stands.
`rgba8`, `bgra8` and `r8` have storage (no atomics); `bgra8srgb` has none; `r8uint`, `r8sint`, `r16uint` and `r16sint` are storage images only.
Out of range values stored to an 8 or 16-bit integer storage image saturate, and a store, an atomic or a load out of bounds reads zeros (alpha 1) and changes no memory.
A texel buffer is a Buffer descriptor (type 9, buffer type 3: the view's address, its range in bytes, the texel's size as the stride, and in word 7 the format's 22-bit pixel format word) in the same table 2, after the storage images: slots 1024 to 2047 hold the uniform texel buffers (the heap's binding 3) and 2048 to 3071 the storage ones (binding 4), so the shaders' handle for element 0 of binding 3 is table 2, index 1024, and of binding 4 index 2048.
The formats with texel buffers (both kinds) are the one to four-component 8, 16 and 32-bit normalised, integer and float formats the specification's table names, `rgb10a2unorm`, `rg11b10float` and `rgb9e5`, and `bgra8`; `rgba8srgb` and `bgra8srgb` have none.
No format has `storageTexelBufferAtomic` yet: shader atomics need GPU-cacheable memory (BUGS).
A fetch of a format that has fewer than four channels reads (R, 0, 0, 1) or (R, G, 0, 1), as the board shows the hardware fills them, and a view never written reads zeros.
The heap has 4096 texture slots and 16 sampler slots.
`writeTexture` and `writeSampler` keep MaliCore's descriptors (malicore(2)) in the device's copy of the heap, the samplers first and then the textures, and a pass whose fragment shaders read the heap is given a copy of it as it stands when the pass is built, as table 1 of its resource table: a later write reaches later passes only.
A sampler has the filter (the same for magnifying and minifying), the address mode on all three axes (`clampToEdge`, `repeat` or `mirroredRepeat`), no mipmaps and a transparent black border.
Only the fragment stage reads the heap, with a texture array at binding 0 and a sampler array at binding 1 of set 0, as Loinnir's shaders do.
A pipeline's blend is the fixed-function stage's: `opaque` replaces the target's colour, `alpha` is source alpha over.
On a `bgra8srgb` target the stage decodes the destination, blends in light and encodes the result, and a clear is encoded too; the fragment shader's colour is light.
A draw of fewer than three vertices or of no instances makes nothing.

Its `DeviceFeatures` are `sampledImages`, `multipleDrawsPerPass`, `indexedDraw`, `indirectDraw`, `indirectDispatch`, `loadAttachments`, `storageImages`, `uniformTexelBuffers`, `storageTexelBuffers`, `timestampQueries` and `occlusionQueries`, and every other flag is false; its formats are `rgba8` and `bgra8` (sampled, filtered, drawn into, blended and storage), `bgra8srgb` (the same but for storage) and `r8` (sampled, filtered and storage).
What it refuses, each with words and `.unsupported`: images that are depth, stencil, compressed or of another format than those four, with mips, layers, cubes, volumes or samples, or 1D (`images1D`); views but of the whole image, and a view that maps components (`imageSwizzle`); a sampler's own border colour (`customBorderColor`); a sampler with separate filters, per-axis addressing, a border, mips, anisotropy or a comparison; textures in compute shaders and in the vertex stage; a one-channel render target; atomics on storage images and texel buffers; pipeline state beyond the two blends and the triangle list (depth and stencil, culling, polygon modes, depth bias, multisampling, blend factors and masks, logic operations, more than one colour target, other topologies, other stages, specialization constants, dynamic state); the commands the features above name (image copies, blits, resolves, fills, updates, clears, clearing attachments, regions, counted draws, statistics queries); more than sixteen draws in a pass, an indirect draw's `drawCount` draws among them; a pass that has several attachments, depth, layers or a view mask; a viewport or scissor but the whole target; import.

